MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: e9620f8c34305754d8cc9a7e49e852d9, type: 3}
  m_Name: FunFacts_TranslationTable_en
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Localization::UnityEngine.Localization.Tables.StringTable
  m_LocaleId:
    m_Code: en
  m_SharedData: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 08a12ef125354d4479d8ebf767c78d49, type: 2}
  m_Metadata:
    m_Items: []
  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 55498452992
    m_Localized: 'Aquamarine is the birthstone for March. Aquamarine''s name comes
      from the Latin for "seawater".

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498452993
    m_Localized: Aragonite is the main mineral that makes up pearls.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498452994
    m_Localized: "The deep blue of azurite comes from copper. Azurite was used to
      create blue paint in the Middle Ages and Renaissance. Azurite is often found
      with Malachite, another copper mineral. \r\n"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498452995
    m_Localized: 'Barite is named from the Greek word, barys, meaning "heavy" in
      reference to its unusually high density. Some specimens of barite can fluoresce,
      or glow, under UV light. '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498452996
    m_Localized: 'Cut and polished benitoite is the official state gem of California.

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498452997
    m_Localized: Calcite is the main mineral that makes up limestone.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498452998
    m_Localized: "The name chrysocolla is from the Greek words chrysos, meaning \u201Cgolden,\u201D
      and kolla, meaning \u201Cglue\u201D."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498452999
    m_Localized: Yellow quartz is called citrine.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453000
    m_Localized: 'The addition of cobalt turns calcite pink. Cobaltoan calcite can
      be easily scratched and dissolves in weak acids like vinegar. '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453001
    m_Localized: 'Cuprite is known as "ruby copper" for its intense red color. Cuprite
      is mined for its high copper content. Cuprite has been used to color glass
      a deep ruby red since the 1st and 2nd millennia BCE. '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453002
    m_Localized: When heated, dioptase generates an electric charge.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453003
    m_Localized: 'Elbaite is pyroelectric, meaning it develops a temporary voltage
      when heated. Single crystals of elbaite can show several colors, making it
      possible to cut unique multicolored gems. "Watermelon" elbaites have a pink
      center surrounded by a green exterior.

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453004
    m_Localized: 'Emerald is the birthstone for the month of May. In most cases,
      high quality emeralds are rarer than diamonds. Natural emeralds almost always
      have inclusions - other materials trapped inside the crystal during formation. '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453005
    m_Localized: 'Fluorite comes in every color of the rainbow!

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453007
    m_Localized: "Commonly known as 'rock salt', halite is the same salt you use
      for cooking! Halite forms from the evaporation of seawater or brine pools.
      Called \"white gold\", salt was once hard to come by and highly sought after. 
      Salt was used as preservative before the invention of refrigeration. \r\n"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453008
    m_Localized: 'Hematite is one of the most abundant sources of iron ore on Earth.The
      name hematite is from the Greek word, haima, for "blood", likely in reference
      to the red streak and powder it produces. Hematite is the main ingredient in
      ocher, a red pigment used to paint rock art thousands of years ago. '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453009
    m_Localized: 'The FIFA World Cup Trophy designed in 1974 has two bands of malachite
      on its base. Malachite is a vibrant green color and has been used as a green
      pigment for thousands of years. Malachite is more common as a cabochon (smoothly
      polished rather than a faceted) gemstone. '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453010
    m_Localized: Morganite is the pink variety of the mineral beryl.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453011
    m_Localized: Because of its shiny, metallic yellow color, pyrite is often called
      "fool's gold". Pyrite can grow in perfect cubes. Pyrite is a replacement mineral
      in many marine fossils.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453012
    m_Localized: 'Pyromorphite typically fluoresces yellow to orange under ultraviolet
      light.

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453013
    m_Localized: "Quartz is one of the most common minerals in the Earth's crust.
      Quartz is the major component of beach sand. Pure quartz is colorless. Purple
      quartz is called amethyst. The early Greeks called quartz krystallos, which
      means \"ice\", and is the origin of the word crystal. \r\n"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453014
    m_Localized: 'Polished slices from a rhodochrosite stalactite show patterns similar
      to tree rings. Rhodochrosite is the official state mineral of Colorado. Rhodochrosite
      is sought after for its natural vibrant pink color.

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453015
    m_Localized: 'Pink quartz is called rose quartz.

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453016
    m_Localized: 'Rubies get their red color from trace amounts of chromium. The
      name ruby comes from the Latin, ruber, meaning "red". Rubies fluoresce under
      ultraviolet, so their natural color is enhanced in sunlight. '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453017
    m_Localized: "Scheelite brightly fluoresces under ultraviolet light. Scheelite
      is one of the primary ores for tungsten, a hard and wear-resistant metal used
      in mining. Scheelite can form as intergrown twins, where two crystals grow
      together in a symmetrical fashion.\_"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453018
    m_Localized: 'Rare gemstone-quality serandite is only found in Quebec, Canada.

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453020
    m_Localized: 'When cut, sodalite may release a "rotten egg" smell due to traces
      of water and sulfur.

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453021
    m_Localized: Stibnite is heavy for its size, but also very soft, making it challenging
      to move without damaging it.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453023
    m_Localized: "Tanzanite, a variety of Zoisite, is named after the only place
      it is found - Tanzania! Tanzanite shows different colors - from blue to violet
      to deep purple - depending on the direction you view it! Tanzanite was added
      to the list of December birthstones in 2002. \r\n"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453024
    m_Localized: 'The rarest colors of topaz are pink and reddish orange hues, known
      as Imperial Topaz. / Blue topaz is the Texas state gemstone!

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453025
    m_Localized: 'Vanadinite contains toxic elements vanadium and lead.

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453026
    m_Localized: 'Vivianite darkens when exposed to sunlight, turning a deep blue-green
      to purplish-black. Vivianite has been found growing on the body of a 5,000-year-old
      glacier mummy. Vivianite was first discovered in 1817 in Cornwall, England. '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 55498453027
    m_Localized: 'Wulfenite is the official state mineral of Arizona.

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  references:
    version: 2
    RefIds: []
